        link: "https://www.dallasnews.com/opinion/letters-to-the-editor/2026/02/04/letters-to-the-editor-death-penalty-canada-epstein-history-lemon-tax-deadline/",
        title:
          "Letters to the Editor - Death penalty, Canada, Epstein, history, Lemon, tax deadline",
        description: "Letters to the Editor",
        content:
          "Re: “I still miss my daddy,” Jan. 22 news story. The symbolic declaration of innocence by Dallas County commissioners for Tommy Lee Walker is late. He was executed in 1956. I supported the death penalty as an adult in my 30s, thinking that it was justified and a deterrent. My dad, who worked all his life for the federal prison system, did not. His reasoning was you could execute an innocent man, and you can’t give him back his life. My father obviously was right. How sad for the Walker family and maybe for others in the same situation. God only knows how many. Please, abolish the death penalty. Katherine Creech, Garland The Republican leadership is now letting President Donald Trump pressure Canada over certification of the Gulfstream aircraft by threatening tariffs and decertifying the Canadian Bombardier Global Express aircraft. Large portions of the Bombardier Global Express were designed and built by many of us in the Dallas-Fort Worth area. The entire wing is in production at the Red Oak facility, just south of Dallas. Bombardier hires over 500 engineers, mechanics and other employees here to produce the Global Express aircraft. These workers want politicians to solve problems, not create them. I’ve worked in the Dallas-Fort Worth area in the aerospace industry for over 30 years, so here’s a helpful hint: You don’t want incompetent politicians certifying passenger aircraft or getting involved in the certification process in any way. Mark Bauer, Haslet Here we are in 2026, and the Jeffrey Epstein hand-wringing continues. Does anyone remember Bill Clinton? The Dems, in their party loyalty, were setting the bar so low a 1-year-old could step over it in overlooking and excusing the poor judgment and indiscretions of then-President Clinton. I recall, “He only lied about sex. Everyone lies about sex.” Just as a rising tide raises all ships, so must a low bar for sexual indiscretions lower the bar for all. I have to chuckle when I hear Democrats assert that what they perceive as vindictive behavior on the part of President Donald Trump is wrong, all while they vindictively try to embarrass any Republican who, decades ago, had contact with Epstein. Hypocrisy continues to reign supreme in the world of American politics. Ted Gold, Plano Re: “Speakers push for inclusion,” Friday Metro&Business story. Evidently the State Board of Education is trying to rewrite history. According to the testimony from SMU doctoral student Jennifer Blanchard Sayed, the new social studies focus is on European achievements to the exclusion of the Islamic world. As much as many News readers hated taking algebra, a Persian, believed to be a Muslim, is considered the father of algebra. Without algebra, we’d probably still be living in caves, fingerpainting the walls with images of our hunts for dinner. Paul Sokal, Dallas/Forest Hills Last month, I was shocked and disturbed by a letter here detailing how the Trump administration took Black holidays off the National Park Free Days list. Well, they are at it again, and again. Recently, the administration removed two memorial displays at an American cemetery in the Netherlands, honoring Black veterans who helped liberate Europe from the Nazis, and replaced them with a tribute to a white soldier. The Dutch were offended to the point where they re-created the panels and posted them outside the cemetery. Europeans understand the value of history. Similarly, the feds removed panels from the President’s House exhibit in Philadelphia that detailed the history of nine slaves held by George Washington. Now Trump officials have ordered the removal of displays in national parks related to the mistreatment of Native Americans. His executive order says these moves will “restore truth and sanity to American history.” How is hiding our history truthful? This is the opposite of sanity. George Orwell said it best, “The most effective way to destroy people is to deny and obliterate their own understanding of their history.” When will Congress stop this erasure of minority history? Ted Felinski, Lewisville Re: “Ex-CNN anchor arrested, released,” Saturday news story. Seeing the former CNN reporter admit to turning the cameras off while church protest planners in St. Paul, Minn., gave “critical information” to others is everything you need to know. Don Lemon was not doing journalism; he was actively assisting the protesters. You don’t get to invade people’s private space and interrupt a constitutionally protected religious service. That’s not journalism. That’s a crime. Frank Randolph, Dallas Re: “More time to pay your property tax,” Friday Metro&Business story. The article explains how the final day to pay your property taxes was Monday because the last day of January was a Saturday, not because of the weather or limited mail delivery. State law does not allow extensions due to weather problems, but all the Collin County offices were closed this past Monday-Wednesday by County Judge Chris Hill, so no one could come to the office and pay their taxes if they wanted to. If the law doesn’t allow for extensions due to extreme bad weather, then there should be a law that the offices are always open. Not everyone can pay online or by phone, as shown by the 40 people standing in line Thursday morning to pay their taxes on time. Do better, Collin County. Jodie Pennington, Murphy We welcome your thoughts in a letter to the editor. See the guidelines and submit your letter here . If you have problems with the form, you can submit via email at letters@dallasnews.com",
