        description:
          "Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman has strongly defended the new India-US trade agreement. She assured that Indian interests, especially in agriculture and dairy, are protected. Tariffs on Indian exports to the US have seen a significant reduction. Opposition parties have raised concerns about transparency and potential impacts. Full details are expected to be shared through parliamentary channels soon.",
        content:
          "Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman has strongly defended the recently announced India-US trade agreement, dismissing opposition allegations of lack of transparency while assuring that Indian interests, particularly in agriculture and energy sectors, remain protected.Key details of the trade agreementIn an exclusive interview with Times Now's Navika Kumar, Sitharaman confirmed that tariffs on Indian exports to the US have been reduced from 50% to 18%, marking a significant improvement for Indian manufacturers. However, she acknowledged that complete details of the agreement are yet to be publicly disclosed. The commerce minister was scheduled to give a suo moto statement in the Lok Sabha at 4:30 PM, but the house was adjourned due to disturbances, Sitharaman explained, adding that full details would be made available through proper parliamentary channels.Agriculture and dairy sectors protectedAddressing concerns about market access for US farm products, the Finance Minister provided assurances while admitting limited knowledge of specific deal terms. I will assure you that farmers' interest, dairy interests are intact, Sitharaman stated, clarifying that red lines were established before negotiations began to protect these sensitive sectors.Commerce Minister Piyush Goyal separately confirmed that the agriculture and dairy sectors would be protected, though detailed specifics remain pending.Energy security questionsOn the critical issue of oil imports, particularly speculation about reduced purchases of cheaper Russian crude in favor of supplies from the US and Venezuela, Sitharaman deflected specific commitments but emphasized Prime Minister Modi's track record. All these months, in fact years, has not Prime Minister Modi taken every decision keeping India's interest top of the priority, she said, referencing India's independent stance during previous international sanctions.Parliamentary disruptions delay disclosureThe Finance Minister attributed delays in sharing agreement details to parliamentary disruptions, with eight opposition MPs suspended following disturbances that included tearing and throwing of papers in the house. You wait for an official clear reply. Suo moto statement he was going to give, Sitharaman said, criticizing the opposition for not allowing Commerce Minister Goyal to present details in parliament before demanding answers.Opposition raises surrender allegationsLeader of Opposition Rahul Gandhi has termed the deal surrender 2.0, questioning why major announcements affecting India came first from Washington rather than New Delhi. The opposition has raised concerns about:Lack of transparency in deal negotiationsPotential impact on Indian agricultureEnergy security implicationsMarket access commitments to the USTimeline for full disclosure unclearWhen pressed on when complete details would be available, Sitharaman indicated they would emerge through parliamentary processes and public documents, though no specific timeline was provided. These are not things which are going to remain in closed files. They are public documents, she assured, adding that implementation timelines for various provisions would also be disclosed.Political Tensions EscalateThe Finance Minister's remarks reflect deepening tensions between the government and opposition, with both sides accusing each other of disrupting parliamentary proceedings and failing to engage constructively on matters of national importance.As India's business community awaits clarity on the trade deal's specifics, the political standoff threatens to delay comprehensive understanding of what could be one of India's most significant trade agreements in recent years.",
