          "The US and India are finalising a trade deal that cuts tariffs, eases technical barriers, and expands market access, though some farm protections will remain.",
        content:
          "The Trump administration is moving closer to formalising a major trade understanding with India, as officials work to convert broad commitments into a written agreement. The proposed deal signals a significant shift in tariff structures, promises expanded market access for American goods, and reflects deeper strategic alignment between Washington and New Delhi. The deal also includes zero tariffs from India on some American exports like wine and nuts.US Trade Representative Jamieson Greer said on Tuesday that the administration is finalising the details of the trade deal with India. Once completed, the agreement would lower Indian tariffs on American industrial products to zero, down from an average of 13.5 per cent. Several duties are expected to be eliminated, although India will retain certain safeguards for sensitive agricultural imports.Greer noted that while some areas of India’s farm sector will remain protected, tariffs on a wide range of agricultural products will be scrapped. He said tariffs “for a variety of things... tree nuts, wine, spirits, fruits, vegetables, etc, they're going down to zero.” However, he did not refer to commodities such as rice, beef, soyabeans, sugar, or dairy, items that India has also kept outside its trade pact with the European Union.Technical Barriers And Standards RecognitionBeyond tariffs, the agreement addresses long-standing technical barriers to trade. Greer confirmed that the two sides had reached an “understanding and an agreement with the Indians as well on a variety of technical trade barriers, areas where they have not accepted US standards. We know American goods are safe.”He explained that the framework would include “a process for recognising US standards,” while acknowledging that India would still need to navigate its internal political and regulatory procedures before implementation. According to Greer, this step could unlock access to a consumer market of more than one billion people for US exporters.Trade Balance, Energy Shifts, And What Comes NextOn the US side, the deal would also involve a reduction in tariffs applied to most Indian goods, with rates expected to fall to 18 per cent from 50 per cent. Greer attributed this adjustment to the scale and rapid expansion of India’s trade surplus with the US, which stood at $53.5 billion in the first 11 months of 2025, up from $45.8 billion in all of 2024.Addressing energy ties, Greer said India is scaling back Russian crude oil imports and is “making the right choice” by diversifying energy purchases toward suppliers such as the US and Venezuela. He added that India had not imported Russian oil before 2022–2023 and has been gradually reducing dependence since late last year.While no timeline has been announced, Greer indicated that formalisation is underway. “We’ll finish papering it, but we know the specifics, we know the details,” he said.",
