        title:
          "India-US trade deal: 15 stocks that could benefit from landmark pact",
        description:
          "India and the US have finalized a landmark trade deal, significantly reducing tariffs on Indian exports to the US from 50% to 18%. This pact is expected to boost several Indian companies, particularly in the textile, auto ancillary, and electronics sectors, by improving their competitiveness and opening new export opportunities.",
        content:
          "Motilal Oswal expects the company’s non-auto exports to benefit, as these do not fall under the Section 232 tariffs of 25%, improving competitiveness in the US market. Around 14% of revenue exposure to the US is likely to benefit directly from the reduction in US tariffs, supporting export growth. The tariff relief is expected to restore competitiveness for Indian textile exporters versus peers such as Bangladesh and Vietnam. Motilal Oswal sees the textile sector as one of the biggest beneficiaries, with Indo Count’s high US revenue share of 65%–90% placing it in a strong position to gain. With a significant share of revenue coming from the US, Welspun Living is expected to benefit from improved pricing power and demand recovery. High dependence on the US market positions the company to gain meaningfully from lower tariff barriers and improved order flows. Motilal Oswal sees Kitex as a key beneficiary, given its ~90% revenue exposure to the US, which could help revive volumes amid large underutilised capacity. With 41% of revenue derived from the US, tariff relief could lead to a 100–200 bps improvement in margins, which had been pressured by higher duties. Analysts expect tariff-related tailwinds to support market share expansion, particularly in export-linked segments. Lower tariffs could help expand market share and increase revenue contribution from exports, currently at 1.5%–2%, while also benefiting from rising orders from data centres, which account for 25%–30% of project business. Around 57% of FY25 sales come from the US, with 20% of manufacturing based there, positioning the company to benefit from improved trade conditions. Motilal Oswal expects new export opportunities to open up, particularly in the mobile handset segment. With tariff support and supply-chain realignment, India could gain share in smartphone assembly, semiconductors (OSAT), and electronics components. This reinforces the PLI-led manufacturing cycle and supports both listed EMS players and ancillary suppliers. From the chemical sector, the stock could be a big beneficiary given its exposure to the US. Lower tariffs are expected to drive margin expansion, as many companies had been absorbing a portion of the 50% duty to remain competitive. With the tariff effectively halved, exporters can reduce prices marginally to regain volumes while retaining a larger share of profits. The company has exposure to the US and multiple international customers. Lower trade barriers also strengthen long-term supplier relationships with US OEMs, providing a meaningful boost to export-led earnings growth. The development is significant for auto ancillary stocks, which have sizeable exposure to the US market. Any reduction in tariffs directly improves price competitiveness, margins and order visibility for Indian exporters. According to a JM Financial report, Sona BLW has a significantly higher exposure to the US at 41%. (Disclaimer: Recommendations, suggestions, views and opinions given by the experts are their own. These do not represent the views of The Economic Times)",
