        article_id: "d596816473b97f66702310049dd1f17c",
        link: "https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/markets/stocks/news/devil-in-the-deal-details-why-it-may-be-too-early-for-investors-to-pop-champagne-on-india-us-trade-pact/articleshow/127899570.cms",
        title:
          "Devil in the deal details? Why it may be too early for investors to pop champagne on India-US trade pact",
        description:
          "India and the US have announced a trade deal. Investors are celebrating, but experts urge caution. The full details are not yet public. Past trade announcements from the US have been inconsistent. Potential costs for switching oil supplies and sensitive sectors like agriculture are concerns. The deal aims to boost trade and investor confidence.",
        content:
          "As the champagne bottles remain uncorked across Dalal Street after US President Donald Trump's announcement of an India-US trade deal with both stocks and rupee soaring, some market insiders are sounding a note of caution. As details of the bilateral trade agreement are yet to be officially announced, the devil may well be lurking in the fine print. The specifics are yet to be known but prima-facie, the 25% additional (penal) tariffs will no longer be levied for buying Russian Oil. The reciprocal tariff will be slashed to 18% instead of 25%. We believe that this is a great boost for the already sombre sentiments. However, one must also remember that exports to the US is a small part of our $4 trillion GDP. So yes the trade deal is good for the economy and markets in the short term but one must not expect miracles out of it,” said Apurva Sheth of SAMCO Securities.Notwithstanding Tuesday’s rally, the analyst said one must look for fresh long builds for the trend to sustain.Dhananjay Sinha, CEO & Co-Head of Institutional Equities at Systematix Group, summed up the trading dynamics: Markets might still applaud the short-term de-escalation. Amid US post-election volatility and India's budget-related setbacks, any tariff relief is appreciated. But the longevity of this enthusiasm depends on the details. History Warns: Trump's Announcements Don't Always Age WellJM Financial struck a cautious note: Wait for fine print: History suggests one needs to be measured in extrapolating trade announcements made by Trump. Over the past year, tariff policy has repeatedly been announced, revised, escalated and then softened, often in response to political/strategic goals. The brokerage cited the US–Korea FTA as a warning: Initial announcements created expectations of stable access, but sector-specific clauses, safeguards and reinterpretations emerged later, diluting benefits and delaying real gains. Recent threats to impose or raise tariffs on the European Union, triggered not by strategic disputes — including tensions linked to Greenland's geopolitical relevance — reinforce the same pattern. Namrata Mittal, CFA, Chief Economist at SBI Mutual Fund, also echoed the sentiment saying these deals are not an agreement. “The threat of tariff imposition on European nations to coerce them to comply on the Greenland issue and then on Korea for a speedy implementation of trade deals is a reminder that exporters and policy makers would always be at their toes when dealing with the US. The $500 Billion QuestionSinha raised red flags about Trump's claimed $500 billion procurement target: Trump's portrayal implies Indian purchases at that level, which doesn't align with existing trade patterns. For 2024–25, India's exports to the US were around $87 billion, with imports at about $45 billion, making total bilateral trade roughly $132 billion. He noted that even the earlier 'Mission 500' aim of $500 billion in combined trade by 2030 was bold. Rephrasing it as solely Indian imports would demand unrealistic expansion, far exceeding the 6.5% average growth seen in the prior six years. Elara Securities added: India agreeing to buy $500 billion of US imports in energy, technology, agricultural, coal claimed by the US President can raise some concern. Russian Crude Exit: Hidden Costs Ahead?Sinha warned that ditching Russian crude won't be painless: Over the last two years, Russian crude has been vital to India's refining sector, enabling access to cheap raw materials and exports of refined fuels, particularly to Europe. Switching to US or Venezuelan supplies would likely increase costs, pressuring domestic refining profits and potentially driving up fuel prices in Europe, which could harm India's over $20 billion in annual refined product exports. Nomura analysts noted that India's oil imports from Russia have been moderating over the last month, mainly due to US sanctions on the two Russian firms, Lukoil and Rosneft. The discount on Russian oil imports has moderated to low single digits, but switching to other energy sources will be slightly more expensive and time-consuming, as refineries may need to be reconfigured, the brokerage said. Increasing US energy imports or imports of Venezuela oil (lower grade than others) may limit gains due to cheaper Russian crude,” Elara Securities added.Agriculture and Defense: The Unspoken Red LinesSinha highlighted sensitive areas conspicuously absent from the announcement: Notably absent is discussion of areas where India has historically set strict boundaries. Agriculture stands out, as eliminating tariffs and barriers there would spark major political and economic backlash. He also raised defense procurement concerns: If the agreement subtly pushes for stronger 'Buy American' obligations, might it urge India to redirect military acquisitions from traditional partners like Russia and France to the US? The tweet provides no details, but this is significant given the magnitude, geopolitical stakes, and extended timelines of defense deals. Mittal cautioned that we think that the risks on other related issues like immigration or service exports is down but not out. India had warmed up to China in recent months. Those developments could continue separately irrespective of the deal. Despite the caveats, analysts acknowledged the sentiment shift. Sujan Hajra, Chief Economist & Executive Director at Anand Rathi Group, noted: The underperformance of Indian equities over the past year can be traced, at least in part, to large and persistent foreign portfolio investor (FPI) outflows. These flows were not driven by corporate fundamentals — which have remained robust — but by rising geopolitical and policy uncertainty around India's trade relationship with the United States. With the India–US treaty now in place, that overhang is beginning to lift. The key shift is not incremental tariff relief, but the restoration of geopolitical and trade stability. As risk premia normalise, India once again looks investable to global capital,” Hajra added.The sentiment was perhaps best captured by Nilesh Shah, MD at Kotak Mahindra AMC, who noted that while devil is in the details, it removes a hanging sword over rupee, equity and rates market. Let us hope that it is a win-win deal for both the countries as they have a lot to gain through cooperation. (Disclaimer: Recommendations, suggestions, views and opinions given by the experts are their own. These do not represent the views of The Economic Times)",
